<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mangkunegaran</h1><p class="st-meta">1757 CE – 1946 CE · capital Surakarta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Javanese princely house founded by the rebel Raden Mas Said, a small but innovative court at Surakarta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-i">Raden Mas Said</a>, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a> prince known as Pangeran Sambernyawa, fought both the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> and the royal courts for years. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-salatiga">Treaty of Salatiga</a> in 1757 he received an apanage carved out of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/surakarta-sunanate">Surakarta Sunanate</a> and the title Mangkunegara I, founding a princely house subordinate in rank but autonomous in practice.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The princes ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puro-mangkunegaran">Puro Mangkunegaran</a> palace in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/surakarta-sunanate">Surakarta</a>. They maintained the Mangkunegaran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, a European-style force formed in 1808. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-iv">Mangkunegara IV</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> estates that made the house wealthy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-vii">Mangkunegara VII</a> was a celebrated patron of dance, music and early radio broadcasting.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/surakarta-sunanate">Surakarta</a> court, Mangkunegaran lost its autonomy in 1946 amid revolutionary opposition to the Javanese nobility, and its lands were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puro-mangkunegaran">Puro Mangkunegaran</a> remains a living cultural centre headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-i">Mangkunegara</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mangkunegaran.jpg" alt="Mangkunegaran" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Lyndonbaines · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mangkunegaran.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1757 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Surakarta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mangkunegara I, Mangkunegara IV, Mangkunegara VII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Praja Mangkunegaran</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=mangkunegaran" aria-label="Mangkunegaran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7745 2460 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mangkunegaran lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8065 2716L8065 2716M8065 2716L7959 2663M8065 2716L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8065 2716h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Surakarta. <a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=mangkunegaran">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
